A1. A manufacturer of thingamajigs and doohinkeys. ~ BOONDOGGLER
A10. Vest protector. ~ COAT
A12. The younger generation skip this. ~ ROPE
A13. A large swimming pool. ~ ERIE
A15. An upturn in architecture. ~ ARCH
A16. Pot steak. ~ ANTE
A17. A reversible handle for a woman. ~ ANNA
A18. It is impossible to make this clear. ~ OPAKE
A19. In the 2B class. ~ IS
A20. Father's appendix. ~ SR
A21. A gate keeper. ~ PETER
A22. A dog protector. ~ SHOE
A24. Direction for printer. ~ STET
A26. What the hitchhikers did between hitches. ~ TROD
A27. Vandyke's birthplace. ~ CHIN
A28. Old-fashioned coloring. ~ BLUSH
A29. This is overdone by poets. ~ OER
A30. A necessary thing to take when traveling. ~ TRIP
A31. Here is poor Art. ~ DAUB
A32. The Doctor's appendix. ~ MD
A33. A member of the scrub team. ~ SOAP
A34. This helped the old bird get a raise. ~ WING
A35. This will get you in Dutch. ~ DU
A36. What they did to poor Art. ~ HUNG
A37. American can. ~ FORD
A38. This plays around the beaches and sometimes gets rough with the bathers. ~ SEA
A39. A doubly blessed event. ~ TWINS
A41. We can't give you much light on this one. ~ DARK
A42. A measure for trouble. ~ PECK
A43. What puzzles do to your brains. ~ RACK
A44. A good place to get a drink. ~ SINK
A45. One of those things we wished we had sold before 1929. ~ SHARE
A46. This reminds one of a Frenchman. ~ IL
A47. Orchestra leader. ~ OR
A48. These were given all the ice cream they could hold at the party. ~ CONES
A49. A dirty dig. ~ SLAM
A52. This is quite touching. ~ FEEL
A55. The beginning of a good fairy story. ~ ONCE
A56. This was worn out by the Romans. ~ TOGA
A57. A deep plot. ~ ACRE
A58. The way deer look from behind. ~ REED
A59. People who travel with dogs. ~ PEDESTRIANS

D1. What the angel did to the Broadway flop. ~ BACKED
D2. The side of the street dangerous to the pedestrian. ~ OTHER
D3. This one died of a severe code. ~ NRA
D4. A gentleman from Spain. ~ DON
D5. Take it or leave it. ~ OPTION
D6. A silly bunch of highfliers. ~ GEESE
D7. The field of poetry. ~ LEA
D8. This old bird is a pioneer in the crossword field. ~ ERN
D9. Takes a shower bath. ~ RINSES
D10. Danced the light fantastic. ~ CAPERED
D11. A dynamic speaker. ~ ORATOR
D14. For land sakes! ~ EARTHQUAKES
D18. Just two glasses and this man makes a spectacle before the eyes of the public. ~ OPTOMETRIST
D22. This subsists on nothing but rolls and water. ~ SHIP
D23. Since prohibition, they can get nothing on this one. ~ HIP
D24. A telephone operator. ~ SLUG
D25. Here is where the rich get soaked. ~ TUB
D27. Where Cliff goes on a jag. ~ CRAG
D28. A bunch of blow-hards. ~ BAND
D30. This makes a weighty matter. (plur.) ~ TONS
D31. It looks daggers in a singular way. ~ DIRK
D33. As Hor. No. 44 looked in the past. ~ SUNK
D34. We used to have this but no beer; now we've got beer but not enough of this. ~ WORK
D35. Decided officially. ~ DECREED
D36. The outcome of the liquor problem. ~ HIC
D37. Part of an old Spanish costume. ~ FAN
D38. Where kindred spirits get together. ~ SEANCE
D40. Sock. ~ WALLOP
D41. This is progressive and straightforward. ~ DIRECT
D42. When the receivers are taken off, these will be ready to talk business. ~ PHONES
D44. In Grandmother's day these were used for "setting-up" exercises. ~ SOFAS
D45. A sophomoric attitude toward a freshman. ~ SCORN
D50. This is hard on that school-girl complexion. ~ AGE
D51. This one has a crazy definition. ~ MAD
